  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 341 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nActive weather returns for the beginning of next week bringing \nincreased moisture and precipitation chances. A frontal boundary \nwill push through Nebraska on Monday resulting in some rain and \nthunderstorm chances by the evening. As this time, the severe threat \nremains marginal which correlates well to the latest SPC guidance. \nIf thunderstorms can develop, the environment will remain favorable \nfor some stronger to briefly severe thunderstorms. Otherwise, \nexpecting these storms to remain fairly isolated especially to the \nsouth and west where less instability is expected. Another round of \nthunderstorms returns on Tuesday evening with another potential for \nsome severe storms. Storms on Tuesday will have a better chance at \nbecome widespread, impacting much of the region. Although the severe \nrisk does remain uncertain at this time, would not be surprised to \nsee some isolated severe storms with large hail and damaging winds \nas the main threats. Near-daily precipitation chances continue \nthrough the rest of next week, but the severe potential as well as \ndevelopment is uncertain. \n\nFor temperatures, the cold front pushing through early week will \nusher in some cooler air into the region. Highs will drop from the \nlow 90s to low 100s on Monday into the low 70s to low 80s on \nTuesday. Temperatures will gradually warm through the end of the \nweek, returning to the 90s by Friday. Overnight lows will also drop \nfrom the mid 60s to low 70s Monday morning to the 50s by Tuesday \nmorning. They will also slowly increase back into the 60s by Friday \nmorning.",
